WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.713
Weak RNG problems often enter through convenience.

2
00:00:02.713 --> 00:00:06.977
A developer adds a timestamp seed to make a warning disappear.

3
00:00:06.977 --> 00:00:09.690
A test hook remains enabled outside tests.

4
00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:15.503
A general-purpose PRNG is reused for a security token because it is already nearby.

5
00:00:15.503 --> 00:00:21.317
A generated secret is printed to logs during troubleshooting and the log path becomes permanent.

6
00:00:21.317 --> 00:00:27.906
These changes may look small in review, but they can change the security meaning of a value.

7
00:00:27.906 --> 00:00:36.433
Code review should flag custom RNGs, fixed seeds, time-based seeds, counters used as security randomness, modulo mapping for bounded values, unsafe

8
00:00:36.433 --> 00:00:39.534
fallback behavior, and random values printed to logs.

9
00:00:39.534 --> 00:00:48.836
Reviewers should also ask whether the call site has identified the value's purpose: token, key, nonce, IV, salt, challenge, or non-security random

10
00:00:48.836 --> 00:00:49.223
choice.

11
00:00:49.223 --> 00:00:56.200
Dependency review should confirm that cryptographic libraries and platform APIs are maintained, appropriate for the deployment environment, and

12
00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:58.138
configured according to organizational policy.

13
00:00:58.138 --> 00:01:02.401
Operational governance keeps these decisions from living only in individual memory.

14
00:01:02.401 --> 00:01:09.378
Document approved RNG sources, security-sensitive use cases, test-only sources, configuration rules, logging restrictions, and ownership for

15
00:01:09.378 --> 00:01:10.153
dependency updates.

16
00:01:10.153 --> 00:01:18.680
Monitoring should focus on safe signals, such as configuration drift, error rates from RNG wrappers, or unexpected use of disabled weak modes,

17
00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:20.230
without exposing generated secrets.

18
00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:24.494
When weak patterns are found, treat them as tracked engineering defects.

19
00:01:24.494 --> 00:01:29.920
Secure randomness is not a one-time utility selection; it is a lifecycle commitment.
